    "['no shutdown', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 disable false', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 disable false\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 1 disable false\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- Juniper uses `disable` instead of `shutdown`, where `false` means enable the interface (equivalent to Cisco's `no shutdown`).\\n- The interface naming follows Juniper's pattern (`ge-1/0/x` for GigabitEthernet).\\n- The `unit` subinterface notation is explicitly declared in Juniper (`.1` becomes `unit 1`).\\n- Two commands are needed since the original configuration applies `no shutdown` to two different interfaces/subinterfaces.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 disable false'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 1 disable false', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 1 disable false\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 1 disable false\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- Juniper uses `disable` instead of `shutdown`, where `false` means enable the interface (equivalent to Cisco's `no shutdown`).\\n- The interface naming follows Juniper's pattern (`ge-1/0/x` for GigabitEthernet).\\n- The `unit` subinterface notation is explicitly declared in Juniper (`.1` becomes `unit 1`).\\n- Two commands are needed since the original configuration applies `no shutdown` to two different interfaces/subinterfaces.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 1 disable false'}]]": 1,
