    "['no shutdown', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'delete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/1.1 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\ndelete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/1.1 disable\\ndelete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/2.1 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, enabling an interface (equivalent to Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\") is done by removing the \"disable\" statement from the interface configuration. The \"delete\" command is used to remove configuration elements. The above translation shows how to enable both interfaces mentioned in the Cisco configuration.', 'trans_template': 'delete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/1.1 disable'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'delete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/2.1 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\ndelete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/1.1 disable\\ndelete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/2.1 disable\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, enabling an interface (equivalent to Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\") is done by removing the \"disable\" statement from the interface configuration. The \"delete\" command is used to remove configuration elements. The above translation shows how to enable both interfaces mentioned in the Cisco configuration.', 'trans_template': 'delete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/2.1 disable'}]]": 1,
